## 1. Archway material registry

All materials of archways, tiny windows, windowsills and construction frames are listed in one file:
`assets/confession/config/archway-materials.json`. It has three lists:

| List | Contents | Used for |
|---|---|---|
| `rims` | items or blocks used as **rim stones** | rim of archways, frames, tiny windows and windowsills |
| `fills` | blocks used to **fill** (finish) an archway | the wall part of archways, tiny windows and windowsills |
| `woods` | **boards** for the door lintel | archways with a door lintel |

### Entry fields

```json
{ "code": "game:cobblestone-{rock}", "id": "cobblestone-{rock}", "texture": "game:block/stone/cobblestone/{rock}1",
  "ledgeTexture": ["game:block/stone/rock/{rock}3", "game:block/stone/rock/{rock}1"] }
```

| Field | Required | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `code` | yes | Block or item code pattern. Placeholders like `{rock}` capture one part of the code, `*` matches anything. `game:cobblestone-{rock}` matches `game:cobblestone-granite` with `rock = granite`. |
| `id` | yes | Material id. May use the placeholders of `code`. **The id is saved in the world** (in every archway built with it). Allowed characters: `a-z 0-9 - _`.  Create a unique ID with your moddomain|
| `texture` | yes | Texture of the material, with domain, without `textures/` and `.png`. |
| `ledgeTexture` | no | Fills only: texture of the ledge of the big 3×3 archways, usually the raw rock version of the fill. String or list, the first existing texture is used. Default: `texture`. |
| `buildable` | no | Default `true`. `false` keeps the look of existing archways, but the material can no longer be used for new ones. Use this instead of removing an entry. |

Notes:

- A block or item belongs to an entry only if the entry's texture **exists**. Codes without a texture are skipped and reported in the server log.
- If several entries match the same block or item, the **first** one wins. Entries added by patches come after the built-in ones.
- Entries **without** `code` are legacy-only: they keep the look of archways built with older versions and can not be built.
- Wood ids are plain wood names without a mod prefix (`oak`, `larch`), because the lintel name and texture use the vanilla wood names.

The server log shows the result after loading, for example:

```
[MedievalArchitecture] Archway materials (server): 50 rims, 585 fills, 14 woods from 25 entries. Skipped: 0 (missing texture), 0 (invalid), 0 (id conflict).
```

### Adding materials of your mod

Add entries with a JSON patch and `dependsOn`, so the patch is only applied if your mod is installed. Put the file into **your** mod (or send it to us, then it goes to `assets/confession/patches/archway-<modid>-compatibility.json`).

```json
[
  {
    "op": "add", "path": "/fills/-", "file": "confession:config/archway-materials.json",
    "dependsOn": [{ "modid": "mymod" }],
    "value": { "code": "mymod:limestonebricks-{color}", "id": "mymod-limestonebricks-{color}", "texture": "mymod:block/stone/limestonebricks/{color}" }
  },
  {
    "op": "add", "path": "/woods/-", "file": "confession:config/archway-materials.json",
    "dependsOn": [{ "modid": "mymod" }],
    "value": { "code": "mymod:plank-{wood}", "id": "{wood}", "texture": "game:block/wood/debarked/{wood}" }
  }
]
```

Fill and rim ids of other mods should start with the mod id (`mymod-…`), so they never collide with vanilla ids.

### When nothing is needed

If your mod adds **variants of vanilla blocks** in the `game` domain and ships their textures under the vanilla paths, the built-in entries already cover them. Examples: new rocks added to `game:worldproperties/block/rock.json` (with `game:stone-<rock>`, `game:cobblestone-<rock>` … and textures under `game:block/stone/…`), or new daub colors as variants of `game:daub-…`. Geology Additions and Material Needs: Geology work this way.

### Names

Archway names show the fill material, e.g. "Small archway (Granite cobblestone)".

- Vanilla fill ids are vanilla block codes, so the vanilla block name (`game:block-<id>`) is used.
- Fills of other mods need a lang entry `confession:archwayfill-<id>`, otherwise "Modded material" is shown:
  ```json
  "confession:archwayfill-mymod-limestonebricks-red": "Red limestone bricks"
  ```
- Rim stones are named with `game:rock-<id>`, woods with `game:material-<id>`.

## 2. Where the registry is used

| Block | Rim stones | Fill | Lintel |
|---|---|---|---|
| Construction frames → archways | added in the world (registry `rims`) | added in the world (registry `fills`) | added in the world (registry `woods`) |
| Tiny windows | from the recipe | added in the world | – |
| Windowsill | from the recipe | added in the world | – |

The recipes of tiny windows and windowsills use `game:stone-*`. Stones of other mods in the `game` domain work automatically. **Only if your mod has stones in its own domain** (`mymod:stone-*`) you need a recipe patch, and the stones also need a `rims` entry.

## 3. Creative stacks per rim stone

Blocks whose look depends on the rim stones only exist as item stacks with attributes. The handbook only knows stacks that are listed in the creative inventory, so the tiny windows and the windowsill create their creative stacks from the rim stone registry when the game loads: one stack per buildable rim stone, also for rocks of other mods.

A block opts in with the attribute `creativeStacksByRim`. The stacks in its JSON are **templates**: their `types` without `rock` are combined with every rim stone.

```json
"attributes": {
  "creativeStacksByRim": true
},
"creativeinventoryStacksByType": {
  "*-north": [{
    "tabs": ["confession", "construction"],
    "stacks": [
      { "type": "block", "code": "arch_tiny-north", "attributes": { "types": { "originblock": "none", "rock": "granite", "style": "a" } } },
      { "type": "block", "code": "arch_tiny-north", "attributes": { "types": { "originblock": "none", "rock": "granite", "style": "b" } } }
    ]
  }]
}
```

- Order: per template, then alphabetically by rim stone id.
- Stacks without `types.rock` are kept as they are.
- If the registry has no rim stones, the JSON stacks stay unchanged.

## 4. Gates, portcullises, drawbridges and big trapdoors

All of them use the block class `BlockGate` with the block entity behavior `Gate`. A gate is placed as one root block plus invisible parts (`confession:gatepart`) and behaves like one big vanilla door.

```json
"class": "BlockGate",
"entityClass": "Generic",
"entityBehaviors": [{ "name": "Gate" }],
"behaviors": [{ "name": "Lockable" }],
"attributes": {
  "reinforcable": true,
  "width": 3, "height": 3, "length": 2,
  "airtight": true,
  "liquidBarrier": true,
  "openSound": "sounds/block/gateopen", "closeSound": "sounds/block/gateclose",
  "animationSpeed": 0.25, "easingSpeed": 2, "easOutSpeed": 1,
  "boxesClosed": [ { "offset": [0, 0, 0], "boxes": [[0, 0, 0.75, 1, 1, 1]] } ],
  "boxesOpen":   [ { "offset": [0, 0, 0], "boxes": [[0, 0, 0, 0.05, 1, 1]] } ]
}
```

| Attribute | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `width`, `height`, `length` | 1 | Size in blocks. The root is the bottom corner where the gate is placed. |
| `boxesClosed`, `boxesOpen` | – | Collision and selection boxes per position. `offset` = `[x, y, z]` from the root, `x` across the width, `y` up, `z` along the length. Boxes are given for a gate facing north and rotated automatically. Positions without an entry have no boxes. |
| `reinforcable` | – | Vanilla reinforcement (plumb and square). Works on every part while sneaking and is stored at the root. |
| `airtight` | `true` | The gate seals rooms (heat retention, e.g. cellars). Like vanilla doors, an airtight gate is a wall also when it is open (unless the world option "open doors not solid" is set). |
| `liquidBarrier` | value of `airtight` | The gate holds back water on every side that is completely covered by a box of the current state. |
| `openSound`, `closeSound` | `game:sounds/block/door` | Sounds, relative to the block's domain. |
| `animationSpeed`, `easingSpeed`, `easOutSpeed` | 1, 10, 10 | Opening animation. The shape needs an animation with the code `opened`. |

A side of a position counts as closed if one box of the current state covers it completely (it does not have to touch it). So rooms and water follow the boxes, e.g. a raised drawbridge is a wall and a lowered one is a floor – no extra settings are needed.

Values of the mod's own gates:

| Block | `airtight` | `liquidBarrier` |
|---|---|---|
| Gate 3t | true | true |
| Gate 4t (peephole) | false | true |
| Drawbridges | false | true |
| Portcullises | false | false |
| Big trapdoors | true | true |

## 5. Rules for addon authors

- **Never rename or remove a material id** after you released it. Archways in existing worlds store the id. Use `"buildable": false` instead.
- The texture paths `confession:block/archway/rim/…`, `…/fill/…`, `…/ledge/…` and `…/wood/…` are **reserved**: the mod registers every material texture under these names at runtime. Do not put your own textures there.
- Do not patch block codes, variant groups or class names of this mod. Existing worlds depend on them.
- Recipes with tools that have no durability (e.g. the plumb and square) need `"consume": false` instead of `"isTool": true`. `isTool` costs durability, and the recipe never matches for an item without it.
